# Proxmox safety

## Gate

Load on every Proxmox mutate turn (power, create/clone, migrate, delete,
resize, disk/network changes, snapshot/backup create-restore-forget,
cloud-init, convert-to-template, guest command). Skip pure inventory
asks (`proxmox-inventory`).

Never invent `node`, `vmid`, `newid`, storage, snapshot name, or backup
volume. Resolve from MCP this turn or from ids the user pasted.

## Prefer MCP (mutate)

When a Proxmox MCP is ready, use these tools only behind an explicit
verb — do not invent a side CLI while MCP works:

| Need | Tools | Gate |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Power on | `proxmox_start_vm` / `proxmox_start_lxc` | explicit start |
| Power off hard | `proxmox_stop_vm` / `proxmox_stop_lxc` | explicit stop |
| Shutdown soft | `proxmox_shutdown_vm` / `proxmox_shutdown_lxc` | explicit shutdown |
| Reboot | `proxmox_reboot_vm` / `proxmox_reboot_lxc` | explicit reboot |
| Pause / resume | `proxmox_pause_vm` / `proxmox_resume_vm` | QEMU only; explicit |
| Create | `proxmox_create_vm` / `proxmox_create_lxc` | explicit create + params |
| Clone | `proxmox_clone_vm` / `proxmox_clone_lxc` | explicit clone + `newid` |
| Migrate | `proxmox_migrate_vm` | explicit migrate + target node |
| Delete guest | `proxmox_delete_vm` / `proxmox_delete_lxc` | explicit delete |
| Resize CPU/mem | `proxmox_resize_vm` / `proxmox_resize_lxc` | explicit resize |
| Disk add/resize/move/remove | `proxmox_add_disk_vm`, `proxmox_resize_disk_*`, `proxmox_move_disk_*`, `proxmox_remove_disk_vm`, `proxmox_add_mountpoint_lxc`, `proxmox_remove_mountpoint_lxc` | explicit disk verb |
| Network add/update/remove | `proxmox_add_network_*`, `proxmox_update_network_*`, `proxmox_remove_network_*` | explicit net verb |
| Snapshot create/rollback/delete | `proxmox_create_snapshot_*`, `proxmox_rollback_snapshot_*`, `proxmox_delete_snapshot_*` | explicit snap verb |
| Backup create/restore/delete | `proxmox_create_backup_*`, `proxmox_restore_backup_*`, `proxmox_delete_backup` | explicit backup verb |
| Cloud-init | `proxmox_set_cloudinit` | explicit cloud-init change |
| Template convert | `proxmox_convert_to_template` | irreversible; explicit |
| Guest shell | `proxmox_execute_vm_command` | explicit command + VMID |

After mutates that return a UPID, prefer `proxmox_get_task_status`
(`proxmox-inventory`) before claiming success.

## Read-only default

List/get status/config/RRD/pools/HA/firewall reads are fine when
Proxmox work is authorized. Do **not**:

- stop / shutdown / reboot / reset / pause
- delete VM or LXC
- migrate / clone / create / convert-to-template
- resize CPU, memory, or disks (especially shrink)
- change cloud-init or network in a way that drops management access
- restore from backup onto a live guest
- run arbitrary guest commands

…unless the user gave an explicit verb this turn naming the VMID (and
node when required).

## Sacred / deny-list guests

If the consuming repo defines sacred VMIDs or deny-lists (for example
storage appliances), **never** power-cycle, migrate, or delete those
guests — even when a broad "fix Proxmox" ask is present. Say the deny
reason and stop.

## Irreversible / blast radius

- Disk shrink, delete guest, restore-from-backup onto a live guest,
  convert-to-template, and cascade-like disk remove: state the risk in
  one sentence; proceed only when clearly authorized.
- Prefer graceful `shutdown_*` over hard `stop_*` unless the user asked
  for force/stop.
- Network/cloud-init edits that can strand management access need the
  user to name the interface/intent; do not invent bridges, VLANs, or
  IPs.
- `proxmox_execute_vm_command` is remote code execution on the guest —
  only the command the user authorized; never dump secrets from output.

## Report shape

1. Node + VMID (+ type)
2. Action taken (start/stop/clone/migrate/…)
3. Task UPID + result when available
4. Post-read status when cheap
5. Stop — do not chain unrelated destructive ops

## Refuse

- Mass power-cycle / delete "all VMs" without each VMID in scope
- Invented `newid`, target node, storage, or cloud-init passwords
- Pasting guest passwords, API tokens, or private SSH keys into chat
